Independent check of the `removeQueries` vs `resetQueries` measurement in https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/pull/11380#issuecomment-5300984911: reproduced, same result. Scratch `QueryObserver` harness against the installed query-core 5.101.4, one subscribed observer on `["auth","session"]`, session value flipped to `null` before the sweep.

| sweep | notifications | observer data after | cache entry |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `removeQueries` | 0 | still `{user:{id:"user-A"}}` | gone |
| `resetQueries` | 3 | `null` | `null` |

So `resetQueries` is the right operation for this sweep, and the "prefer `removeQueries`" advice I had put in #11417 was wrong for the sign-out path. It holds only for a local reset under an observer that stays mounted, which is why #11417 and #11382 still avoid reset.

## One thing worth checking before this lands, because it can undo the sweep

The stale-account data is not only in the query cache. `api.get` coalesces identical in-flight GETs keyed on the request path alone (`coalescedGet` / `inflightGets` in `ui/src/api/client.ts`), and the account-scoped query functions pass no abort signal. A request issued under one session can therefore be joined by a caller that runs after the account changed, and be answered with the previous account's data.

`resetQueries` refetches active observers immediately. If a GET for a swept path was issued under the outgoing session and is still in flight when the sweep runs, that refetch joins it, and the previous account's response is written straight back into the cache that was just cleared.

This is measurable the same way the notification counts were. #11430 adds `detachInflightGet(path)` in `ui/src/api/client.ts` for exactly this case: it drops the in-flight entry so later callers issue a fresh request, while the original still settles for the callers that asked for it — no abort, because those callers still want their answer. If #11430 lands first, the sweep can call it for the paths it clears; if this PR lands first, #11430 rebases onto it.

Wording for a `useSignOut.ts` code comment recording why reset and not removal, if it is useful to carry into this PR. Not opened separately, since this one rewrites those lines.

```ts
// If this grows into a sweep of the account-scoped caches, clear them with
// `resetQueries`, not `removeQueries`. Removal does not notify observers
// that are already subscribed — nothing rebinds them until some other
// change forces a render — so the entry disappears while every mounted
// observer keeps serving the signed-out account's value, and the redirect
// in CloudAccessGate that fires on the session going empty never runs.
// Reset notifies, the refetch returns null, and the app leaves. Measured
// against query-core 5.101.4: removal produced 0 notifications and left
// the observer holding the old session; reset produced 3 and null.
//
// The opposite advice holds for a *local* reset under an observer that
// stays mounted (InviteLanding, the onboarding draft gate): reset rewinds
// the update counters `isFetchedAfterMount` is derived from while that
// observer keeps its bind-time baseline, so the flag can never read true
// again and the gate withholds forever. Those spots avoid reset. Sign-out
// is not one of them: it resets the session too, so those consumers
// redirect, unmount, and remount fresh.
```

